          DIGITAL ALPHASERVER 8400 RUNNING SAP R/3 SOFTWARE
                    SHATTERS TRANSACTION RECORDS


 ...benchmark reveals new plateau of performance and scalability...


Philadelphia, PA/Maynard, MA -- April 12, 1995 -- SAP AG and Digital 
Equipment Corporation today announced the results of the first 
benchmark for SAP's R/3 software on Digital's new AlphaServer 8400 
5/300 system.  The tests show that the R/3 SD (Sales and 
Distribution) benchmark running on Digital UNIX* provides 
transaction throughput equivalent to mainframe-class performance.

     Running on the new Digital AlphaServer 8400 5/300 configured 
with 8 CPUs, 6GB memory, Digital UNIX V3.2 and Oracle V7.1.4, SAP's 
SD (Sales and Distribution) benchmark achieved 36,000 transactions 
per hour with a response time under 1.5 seconds.  This performance 
equates to 300 SD benchmark users supported on a single server.  

     "We are gratified by the flexibility and power 64-bit 
technology provides to our customers," said Paul Wahl, executive 
vice president of marketing for SAP AG. "With the AlphaServer 8400 
we reached a new plateau of performance and scalability for our 
sophisticated mission-critical R/3 applications."

     "With the AlphaServer 8000-series family, we can configure R/3 
systems to support several thousand users, providing a comprehensive 
solution for customers requiring one central integrated database to 
manage extremely large projects with SAP's PS system," said Bertram 
Mandel, Digital's SAP marketing manager.  "Using this combination of 
technologies, customers can maintain millions of accounts 
receivable, update huge numbers of master records, explode complex 
bill of materials in minutes rather than days, and process thousands 
of salary transactions in a few hours.  This is the business 
environment that the AlphaServer 8000 family supports best" 

     "Our customers will continue to benefit from their investment 
in the Alpha technology for their implementation of R/3, with the 
best price/performance in the industry" said Toni Steiner, Digital's 
worldwide SAP relationship manager.  "The family of AlphaServers 
allows R/3 prospects to start and stay with the 64-bit architecture, 
avoiding the inevitable, expensive migrations in the future."

     The benchmarks were run to provide concrete data for high end 
R/3 systems and to identify the specific performance gains based on 
Alpha very large memory database technology.  A team of SAP, 
Digital, and Oracle specialists ran the R/3 SD benchmark -- 
currently the most demanding R/3 application.  The team also will 
benchmark the client/server-based R/3 system as soon as enough 
application servers are available to fully load the AlphaServer 8400 
as a pure database server.  

     SAP provides flexible, integrated client/server and mainframe-
based business applications software that is compatible with most 
popular hardware, software and database platforms.  More than 4,300 
companies in 41 countries use SAP software to manage complex, 
financial, manufacturing, sales and human resources requirements.  
SAP's R/3 client/server suite is installed in 2,900 sites worldwide 
and is accepted as the standard in key industries such as oil, 
chemicals, consumer packaged goods, and high technology/electronics.  
Founded in 1972, SAP AG is headquartered in Walldorf, Germany and 
employs a workforce of over 5,000 worldwide.  SAP America is the 
largest of SAP's 30 global subsidiaries.  SAP America is comprised 
of its corporate headquarters in Philadelphia, a Technology 
Development Center in Foster City, Calif., and sales and support 
offices throughout North America.  

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace. 
